---
title: "Language overview"
description: "Learn the QQL building blocks: scripts, statements, values, vector roles, and strict clause order."
sidebar:
  order: 1
---

QQL is the product boundary between an application intent and a Qdrant
operation. Write a readable statement, inspect its route before execution, and
keep the same source across the CLI, Rust, Python, Node.js, and WebAssembly.

{% glassCard title="The mental model" %}
QQL is not a stringly typed wrapper around REST. A statement is parsed into an
AST, optionally rewritten by the host, planned into a Qdrant operation, and
then executed. The language rejects incomplete or ambiguous input early.
{% /glassCard %}

## Scripts are composed from statements

Keywords are ASCII case-insensitive while identifiers retain their spelling.
Separate statements with semicolons; a final semicolon is allowed. The parser
accepts at most 256 statements in one script.

{% qqlExample title="Provision, write, and retrieve" %}
```sql
CREATE COLLECTION notes (
  dense VECTOR(384, COSINE)
);

UPSERT INTO notes VALUES {
  id: 1,
  text: 'first note'
};

QUERY TEXT 'note'
FROM notes
USING dense
LIMIT 5;
```
{% /qqlExample %}

| Family | Use it for | Statements |
|---|---|---|
| Retrieval | Finding or browsing points | `QUERY`, `SCROLL`, `COUNT` |
| Data | Creating and mutating points | `UPSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, payload/vector operations |
| Schema | Defining Qdrant resources | `CREATE`, `ALTER`, `DROP`, `SHOW` |

## Values and vector roles

QQL supports strings, signed integers, floats, booleans, `null`, ordered
objects, and lists. Point IDs are unsigned integers or strings. Vector shape,
not its field name, determines its role.

| Role | Value shape | Typical use |
|---|---|---|
| Dense | `[0.1, 0.2, 0.3]` | Semantic nearest-neighbor retrieval |
| Sparse | `{indices: [1, 9], values: [0.4, 0.7]}` | Lexical or sparse retrieval |
| Multivector | `[[0.1, 0.2], [0.3, 0.4]]` | Late interaction such as ColBERT |

{% aside type="tip" title="Start with text, add vectors deliberately" %}
`QUERY TEXT` and `UPSERT ... USING` need an embedder at execution time. Use
explicit `VECTOR` values when your application owns embedding generation or
when you want a fully offline planning workflow.
{% /aside %}

## Query clauses have one order

Each clause appears at most once. The parser does not silently reorder a query:

`USING` → `PREFETCH` → `WHERE` → `SHARD` → `PARAMS` → `SCORE THRESHOLD` →
`GROUP BY` → payload/vector selectors → `LIMIT` → `OFFSET`.

{% qqlExample title="A complete nearest-neighbor query" %}
```sql
QUERY TEXT 'search'
FROM docs
USING semantic_v2 AS DENSE
WHERE status = 'published'
SHARD 'acme'
PARAMS (hnsw_ef = 128, exact = false, acorn = true)
SCORE THRESHOLD 0.5
WITH PAYLOAD INCLUDE (title, url)
WITH VECTOR false
LIMIT 20
OFFSET 10;
```
{% /qqlExample %}

The [Queries](/docs/language/queries/) page selects a retrieval expression. The
[Filters](/docs/language/filters/) page covers the `WHERE` predicate language.
